WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:09.424
This video covers one of the most important
connections on the job: the 144-fiber trunk at

2
00:00:09.424 --> 00:00:11.525
the top of rack switch.

3
00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:19.724
We will cover what the number one forty-four
counts, what odd means here, the rule for

4
00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:25.697
every leg, a complete example, both ends of
the trunk, and common mistakes.

5
00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:36.684
You may have heard this described as one
forty-four fiber to the odd rows, ports one to

6
00:00:36.684 --> 00:00:37.548
thirty-one.

7
00:00:38.298 --> 00:00:40.623
Let us check that against the guide.

8
00:00:42.123 --> 00:00:49.556
The guide says it is the odd-numbered ports on
the top of rack switch: ports one, three,

9
00:00:49.556 --> 00:00:52.427
five, and so on, up to thirty-one.

10
00:00:53.777 --> 00:01:00.394
Those odd ports happen to be the top row of
the switch, which is probably where the word

11
00:01:00.394 --> 00:01:01.521
rows came from.

12
00:01:02.871 --> 00:01:05.346
It is not odd rows of racks.

13
00:01:06.696 --> 00:01:14.388
At the far end of the same trunk, each TPU
rack gets one row on a panel, rows one to

14
00:01:14.388 --> 00:01:14.845
nine.

15
00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:18.049
We will see that later in this video.

16
00:01:22.199 --> 00:01:26.316
One forty-four is the number of fibers in the
trunk.

17
00:01:27.666 --> 00:01:30.119
It breaks out into eighteen legs.

18
00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:35.627
Each leg is an MPO-8 plug holding eight
fibers.

19
00:01:36.377 --> 00:01:40.302
Eighteen times eight is one hundred
forty-four.

20
00:01:41.652 --> 00:01:44.532
Legs one through sixteen are active.

21
00:01:45.882 --> 00:01:48.783
Legs seventeen and eighteen are spares.

22
00:01:52.933 --> 00:02:00.720
This is the top of rack switch, an Arista
7060, on side A of the TPU rack.

23
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.523
It has thirty-two ports arranged in sixteen
cages.

24
00:02:07.273 --> 00:02:11.198
Each cage holds two ports, one above the
other.

25
00:02:12.548 --> 00:02:18.329
The top port in each cage has an odd number:
one, three, five, seven.

26
00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:24.501
The bottom port has an even number: two, four,
six, eight.

27
00:02:25.851 --> 00:02:29.669
Only the odd ports, on the top row, are yours.

28
00:02:30.419 --> 00:02:36.201
The even ports go to the machines inside the
rack, and they are pre-installed.

29
00:02:36.951 --> 00:02:38.551
Do not touch them.

30
00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:44.679
At the right end are two copper ports, ETH and
console.

31
00:02:45.429 --> 00:02:48.096
We will cover those in lesson twelve.

32
00:02:52.246 --> 00:02:53.547
Now the rule.

33
00:02:54.297 --> 00:02:56.580
Leg one goes in port one.

34
00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.298
Leg two goes in port three.

35
00:03:01.048 --> 00:03:02.541
Not port two.

36
00:03:03.291 --> 00:03:07.195
Port two is the even port right below port
one.

37
00:03:08.545 --> 00:03:11.041
Leg three goes in port five.

38
00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:16.933
Keep going in order: leg N goes in the Nth odd
port.

39
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:20.480
There is a quick way to work it out.

40
00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:25.646
Multiply the leg number by two, then subtract
one.

41
00:03:27.446 --> 00:03:32.929
Leg seven: two times seven is fourteen, minus
one is thirteen.

42
00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.108
Port thirteen.

43
00:03:36.458 --> 00:03:42.602
Leg sixteen: two times sixteen is thirty-two,
minus one is thirty-one.

44
00:03:43.352 --> 00:03:47.021
Port thirty-one, the top port of the last
cage.

45
00:03:48.371 --> 00:03:55.633
The guide checked this rule against every TPU
rack in this hall: all four hundred thirty-two

46
00:03:55.633 --> 00:03:56.265
of them.

47
00:04:00.415 --> 00:04:04.148
Here are all sixteen legs with their colors
and ports.

48
00:04:04.898 --> 00:04:07.629
Pause the video if you want to study it.

49
00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:12.293
Notice row five.

50
00:04:13.043 --> 00:04:16.840
Leg thirteen is blue again, just like leg one.

51
00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:22.859
Legs thirteen to sixteen repeat blue, orange,
green and brown.

52
00:04:24.209 --> 00:04:29.777
That is why you always read the leg number on
the label, not just the color.

53
00:04:31.127 --> 00:04:35.330
And at the bottom, legs seventeen and eighteen
are spares.

54
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.597
Nothing plugs into them at the switch.

55
00:04:42.747 --> 00:04:45.243
Let us land a trunk step by step.

56
00:04:46.243 --> 00:04:53.035
First, read the wrap-around label near the
boot, and check it against the cutsheet row

57
00:04:53.035 --> 00:04:54.457
for this TPU rack.

58
00:04:55.807 --> 00:04:57.343
Pick up leg one.

59
00:04:58.093 --> 00:04:59.309
It is blue.

60
00:05:00.059 --> 00:05:03.131
Read its label to confirm it says one.

61
00:05:03.881 --> 00:05:07.230
Scope it, clean it, and plug it into port one.

62
00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.329
Leg two is orange.

63
00:05:11.079 --> 00:05:14.130
It goes in the next odd port, port three.

64
00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.976
Later you pick up another blue leg.

65
00:05:18.726 --> 00:05:20.177
Read the label.

66
00:05:20.927 --> 00:05:22.527
It says thirteen.

67
00:05:23.277 --> 00:05:27.159
Two times thirteen, minus one, is twenty-five.

68
00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:31.411
Legs seventeen and eighteen are spares.

69
00:05:32.161 --> 00:05:36.705
Label them and dress them up so they do not
touch the floor.

70
00:05:38.055 --> 00:05:43.089
And store the trunk slack, above the split
point, on top of the rack.

71
00:05:43.839 --> 00:05:45.333
Never in the tray.

72
00:05:49.483 --> 00:05:56.022
This is the cable we just plugged in, now
followed to its other end: the end of row

73
00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:56.416
rack.

74
00:05:57.816 --> 00:06:00.717
It lands on the front of a large panel.

75
00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:05.457
Each TPU rack gets one row on this panel.

76
00:06:06.207 --> 00:06:10.687
Here, the trunk for the third TPU rack fills
row three.

77
00:06:12.037 --> 00:06:20.188
The legs go left to right: legs one and two in
column A, and so on, up to legs fifteen and

78
00:06:20.188 --> 00:06:21.999
sixteen in column H.

79
00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:28.149
And the spares, legs seventeen and eighteen,
go in column I.

80
00:06:28.899 --> 00:06:32.590
Lesson eight explains this panel in detail.

81
00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.193
Finally, the common mistakes.

82
00:06:40.193 --> 00:06:43.415
A leg in an even port, the bottom row.

83
00:06:44.165 --> 00:06:46.874
Even ports belong to the machines.

84
00:06:47.624 --> 00:06:50.931
Do not disturb them yourself; tell your lead.

85
00:06:52.281 --> 00:06:57.017
Off by one, like leg two in port two, or in
port five.

86
00:06:57.767 --> 00:07:02.567
Check it: the port must equal two times the
leg, minus one.

87
00:07:03.917 --> 00:07:08.610
Mistaking leg thirteen for leg one, because
both are blue.

88
00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.216
Always read the label.

89
00:07:12.566 --> 00:07:14.251
A spare plugged in.

90
00:07:15.001 --> 00:07:19.076
And a leg plugged in without being scoped and
cleaned first.

91
00:07:23.226 --> 00:07:26.021
Now open lesson seven in the course.

92
00:07:26.771 --> 00:07:32.871
You will plug legs into the right ports
yourself, then find the mistake on a switch

93
00:07:32.871 --> 00:07:35.517
that has one leg in the wrong place.

